{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "06a-verified-sort.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "A verified sort check",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Say what it means for a sorting function to be correct, and prove that insertion sort is, for every list.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "An application of lesson 6, outside the course's order: nothing later depends on it. It uses induction on lists, `simp` and `omega` from lessons 5 and 6, and `len` and `count` from lessons 6 and 3; two new steps, a **lemma** and the tactic `split`, are explained where they come up.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Reversing twice",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A warm-up. `rev` reverses a list: the reverse of `x :: xs` is the reverse of `xs` with `x` put at the end.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "def rev : List Nat → List Nat\n  | [] => []\n  | x :: xs => rev xs ++ [x]\n\n#eval rev [1, 2, 3]\n#eval rev (rev [1, 2, 3])",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Reversing twice should give the list back, for every list. Try induction on `xs`: in the `cons x xs` case the goal is `rev (rev xs ++ [x]) = x :: xs`, and the hypothesis `ih : rev (rev xs) = xs` does not apply, because `rev` is applied to `rev xs ++ [x]`, not to `rev xs`. The proof needs a fact about reversing a list with something at its end. A fact proved on the way to another is a **lemma**:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem rev_snoc (xs : List Nat) (x : Nat) : rev (xs ++ [x]) = x :: rev xs := by\n  induction xs with\n  | nil => rfl\n  | cons y ys ih => simp [rev, ih]",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "With the lemma, `simp` can turn `rev (rev xs ++ [x])` into `x :: rev (rev xs)`, and then `ih` finishes it. That is the first exercise.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Insertion sort",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Insertion sort sorts a list the way many people sort a hand of cards: take the cards one at a time and put each into its place among the ones already sorted. `ins x ys` puts `x` into a sorted list `ys`, before the first number at least as big; `isort` does that for each number in turn.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "def ins (x : Nat) : List Nat → List Nat\n  | [] => [x]\n  | y :: ys => if x ≤ y then x :: y :: ys else y :: ins x ys\n\ndef isort : List Nat → List Nat\n  | [] => []\n  | x :: xs => ins x (isort xs)\n\n#eval ins 4 [1, 3, 5, 7]\n#eval isort [5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6]",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "What correct means",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A sort is correct when its output is in order. That alone is not enough:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "def sorted : List Nat → Bool\n  | [] => true\n  | [_] => true\n  | x :: y :: ys => x ≤ y && sorted (y :: ys)\n\ndef lazySort (_ : List Nat) : List Nat := []\n\n#eval sorted (isort [3, 1, 2])\n#eval sorted (lazySort [3, 1, 2])",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`lazySort` throws the list away, and its empty output is sorted. A correct sort must also keep the numbers it was given: the output has the same length as the input, and each number occurs in it as many times as in the input (`count` from lesson 3). Three theorems, each about every list, make the specification:\n\n1. `len (isort xs) = len xs`\n2. `count a (isort xs) = count a xs`, for every number `a`\n3. `sorted (isort xs) = true`\n\nThe first two say `isort` rearranges its input without losing or adding a number; the third that the rearrangement is in order. No number of examples shows them; induction does.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Each theorem through a lemma",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`isort` is built from `ins`, so each theorem about `isort` goes through a lemma about `ins`. `ins` has an `if` in it, and a proof about it needs both branches. The tactic `split` does that: on a goal with an `if`, it gives one goal where the condition holds and one where it does not.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The count lemma, as an example of `split`:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem count_ins (a x : Nat) (ys : List Nat) : count a (ins x ys) = count a (x :: ys) := by\n  induction ys with\n  | nil => rfl\n  | cons y ys ih =>\n    simp only [ins]\n    split\n    · rfl\n    · simp only [count] at *\n      rw [ih]\n      omega",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`simp only [ins]` unfolds one step of `ins`, leaving its `if`; `split` makes the two cases. When `x ≤ y`, `ins` gave `x :: y :: ys`, which is `x :: (y :: ys)`, and `rfl` closes it. Otherwise it gave `y :: ins x ys`; `simp only [count] at *` unfolds `count` in the goal and in `ih` (`at *` means everywhere), `rw [ih]` replaces the left side of `ih` with its right, and `omega` finishes the sums. The bullets `·` mark the two cases.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The sorted lemma has more cases: inserting into a sorted list, the new number may land in front of the first or further along, and the list may be short. Here it is in full; you do not need to follow every line to use it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem sorted_ins (x : Nat) (ys : List Nat) (h : sorted ys = true) : sorted (ins x ys) = true := by\n  induction ys with\n  | nil => rfl\n  | cons y ys ih =>\n    cases ys with\n    | nil => simp only [ins]; split <;> simp [sorted] <;> omega\n    | cons z zs =>\n      simp only [ins]\n      split\n      · simp_all [sorted]\n      · simp only [sorted, Bool.and_eq_true, decide_eq_true_eq] at h\n        have ih' := ih h.2\n        simp only [ins] at ih' ⊢\n        split\n        · simp_all [sorted]; omega\n        · simp_all [sorted]",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The hypothesis `(h : sorted ys = true)` before the colon makes this a statement *if `ys` is sorted*: inserting keeps a sorted list sorted. `have ih' := ih h.2` uses the induction hypothesis on the rest of the list, which `h` says is sorted too.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Each statement is fixed; replace the `sorry`s. The **Lean goals** tab shows what is left at the cursor.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem rev_rev (xs : List Nat) : rev (rev xs) = xs := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove that reversing a list twice gives it back, using the lemma `rev_snoc`.",
      "leanSolution": "  induction xs with\n  | nil => rfl\n  | cons x xs ih => simp [rev, rev_snoc, ih]",
      "leanStart": "  induction xs with\n  | nil => sorry\n  | cons x xs ih => sorry",
      "hints": [
        "The `nil` case computes.",
        "In the `cons` case, give `simp` the definition, the lemma and the hypothesis: `simp [rev, rev_snoc, ih]`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem len_ins (x : Nat) (ys : List Nat) : len (ins x ys) = len ys + 1 := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove that `ins` makes a list one longer.",
      "leanSolution": "  induction ys with\n  | nil => rfl\n  | cons y ys ih =>\n    simp only [ins]\n    split\n    · rfl\n    · simp [len, ih]",
      "leanStart": "  induction ys with\n  | nil => sorry\n  | cons y ys ih =>\n    simp only [ins]\n    split\n    · sorry\n    · sorry",
      "hints": [
        "The `nil` case computes: `len [x]` is `0 + 1`.",
        "When `x ≤ y`, `ins` gave `x :: y :: ys`, and both sides compute to the same number: `rfl`.",
        "Otherwise it gave `y :: ins x ys`: unfold `len` and use `ih`, with `simp [len, ih]`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem len_isort (xs : List Nat) : len (isort xs) = len xs := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove the first theorem of the specification: sorting keeps the length.",
      "leanSolution": "  induction xs with\n  | nil => rfl\n  | cons x xs ih => simp [isort, len_ins, len, ih]",
      "leanStart": "  induction xs with\n  | nil => sorry\n  | cons x xs ih => sorry",
      "hints": [
        "`isort (x :: xs)` is `ins x (isort xs)`, and `len_ins` says how long that is.",
        "`simp [isort, len_ins, len, ih]`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem count_isort (a : Nat) (xs : List Nat) : count a (isort xs) = count a xs := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove the second: sorting keeps how many times each number occurs, using `count_ins`.",
      "leanSolution": "  induction xs with\n  | nil => rfl\n  | cons x xs ih => simp only [isort, count_ins, count, ih]",
      "leanStart": "  induction xs with\n  | nil => sorry\n  | cons x xs ih => sorry",
      "hints": [
        "The same shape as `len_isort`, with `count_ins` in place of `len_ins`.",
        "`simp only [isort, count_ins, count, ih]`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem sorted_isort (xs : List Nat) : sorted (isort xs) = true := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove the third: the output of `isort` is sorted, using `sorted_ins`.",
      "leanSolution": "  induction xs with\n  | nil => rfl\n  | cons x xs ih => exact sorted_ins x (isort xs) ih",
      "leanStart": "  induction xs with\n  | nil => sorry\n  | cons x xs ih => sorry",
      "hints": [
        "`isort (x :: xs)` is `ins x (isort xs)`, and `ih` says `isort xs` is sorted.",
        "`sorted_ins x (isort xs) ih` is exactly the proof: `exact sorted_ins x (isort xs) ih`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> A specification says what a function must do for every input: for a sort, an output in order with the same numbers as the input. Each theorem about `isort` went through a lemma about `ins`, the function it is built from, and `split` took `ins`'s `if` apart. With the three proved, `isort` is correct on every list, which no test suite can show; `lazySort` passes the third theorem and fails the first two.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
